# Example AUN network map for b-em
#
# To use this file, copy it to ~/.config/b-em/AUNmap.cfg and edit it for
# your setup. This file is only read when AUNMODE is enabled in
# econet.cfg.
#
# b-em looks for this file via find_cfg_file("AUNmap", ".cfg"), which
# searches (in order) $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/b-em, $HOME/.config/b-em and the
# current directory.
#
# Each line has the form:
#
#   AddMap <ip-subnet> <econet-network-number>
#
# <ip-subnet> is an IPv4 address; only its first three octets (the /24
# prefix) are used. Any host whose IP address falls within that /24 is
# treated as being on the given Econet network number.
#
# This is used a few ways:
#  - With AUNSTRICT enabled, when b-em needs to send to a destination
#    network/station that isn't listed in econet.cfg, it looks here for
#    an AddMap entry whose network number matches the destination
#    network, and guesses the destination IP as that entry's /24 prefix
#    with the destination station number as the last octet (port 32768).
#  - With LEARN enabled, when b-em receives a packet from an IP address
#    it doesn't recognise, it looks here for an AddMap entry whose /24
#    matches that IP, and uses its network number (with the last octet of
#    the sender's IP as the station number) to add the sender to its
#    station table.
#  - Either way, the entry whose /24 matches b-em's own listening address
#    tells b-em which network number it is itself part of, which is used
#    when AUNSTRICT guesses an address for destination network 0.
#
# Lines not starting with "AddMap" (including comments) are ignored.
#
#
# Example: b-em is on 192.168.1.x, which corresponds to Econet network 0
# (the default - usually fine to leave commented out unless you need to
# be explicit):
#AddMap 192.168.1.0 0
#
# Example: hosts reachable via a PiEconetBridge that exposes several
# Econet networks on different IP subnets:
AddMap 172.17.62.0 62
AddMap 172.17.63.0 63
AddMap 172.17.127.0 127
#
# Note: the "network 1" wired-Econet example in econet.cfg.example has no
# corresponding AddMap entry here, because that segment is reachable via
# the same 192.168.1.x /24 as network 0 - AddMap can't distinguish two
# Econet networks on the same subnet, so that example relies on explicit
# econet.cfg entries rather than AUNSTRICT/LEARN guessing via AddMap.
